ents/totienomial-coefficients","Totienomial coefficients"]],"children":[],"edges":[],"links":{"uses":[],"uses_total":0,"used_by":[["NumberTheory/special-numbers/binomial-coefficients/binomid-indices-and-generalized-binomial-coeffic","Binomid indices and generalized binomial coefficients","Binomial coefficients and factorials",2]],"used_by_total":1},"results":[{"id":"Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/generalized-factorial","title":"Generalized factorial","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.generalizedFactorial","summary":"For a sequence $f \\colon \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ and a natural number $n$, the generalized factorial of $f$ at $n$ is the product $f(1) f(2) \\cdots f(n)$ of the values $f(i)$ for $1 \\le i \\le n$; for $n = 0$ it is the empty product $1$.","route":"../roadmap/Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/index.html#generalized-factorial"},{"id":"Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/is-positive-sequence","title":"Positive sequence","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.IsPositiveSequence","summary":"A sequence $f \\colon \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ is positive when $f(i) > 0$ for every $i > 0$; the value $f(0)$ is unrestricted.","route":"../roadmap/Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/index.html#is-positive-sequence"},{"id":"Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/generalized-factorial-pos","title":"Generalized factorials of positive sequences are positive","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.generalizedFactorial_pos","summary":"Let $f \\colon \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ be a positive sequence, that is $f(i) > 0$ for every $i > 0$. Then for every natural number $n$, the generalized factorial $f(1) f(2) \\cdots f(n)$ is positive.","route":"../roadmap/Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/index.html#generalized-factorial-pos"},{"id":"Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficient","title":"Generalized binomial coefficient","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.generalizedBinomialCoefficient","summary":"Let $f \\colon \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ be a sequence and write $k!_f = f(1) f(2) \\cdots f(k)$ for its generalized factorial, so that $0!_f = 1$. For natural numbers $n$ and $m$, the generalized binomial coefficient of $f$ is the rational number","route":"../roadmap/Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/index.html#generalized-binomial-coefficient"},{"id":"Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficient-mul-den","title":"Defining equation of the generalized binomial coefficient","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.generalizedBinomialCoefficient_mul_denominator","summary":"Let $f \\colon \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ be a positive sequence, that is $f(i) > 0$ for every $i > 0$, and write $k!_f = f(1) f(2) \\cdots f(k)$ for its generalized factorial. Then for all natural numbers $n$ and $m$, the generalized binomial coefficient $\\binom{n+m}{n}_f$, defined as $(n+m)!_f / (n!_f \\, m!_f)$ with division by $0$ giving $0$, satisfies $\\binom{n+m}{n}_f \\cdot n!_f \\, m!_f = (n+m)!_f$ in…","route":"../roadmap/Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/index.html#generalized-binomial-coefficient-mul-den"},{"id":"Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficient","title":"Totienomial coefficient","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.totienomialCoefficient","summary":"Let $\\varphi$ be Euler's totient function and write $k!_\\varphi = \\varphi(1) \\varphi(2) \\cdots \\varphi(k)$. For natural numbers $n$ and $m$, the totienomial coefficient is the generalized binomial coefficient of $\\varphi$, that is the rational number","route":"../roadmap/Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/index.html#totienomial-coefficient"},{"id":"Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/totienomial-integral-factorization","title":"Integrality and prime factorization of totienomial coefficients","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.totienomial_integral_factorization","summary":"For all natural numbers $n$ and $m$, the totienomial coefficient $\\binom{n+m}{n}_\\varphi$ of Euler's totient function $\\varphi$ is a natural number, namely","route":"../roadmap/Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/index.html#totienomial-integral-factorization"}],"result_edges":[["Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficient-mul-den","Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficient",1,0],["Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficient","Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficient",1,0],["Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficient","Combinatorics/binomial-coefficients/generalized-binomial-coefficients/totienomial-coefficients/generalized-factorial",1,0],["Combinatorics/bino