athematics & Computer Science","Discrete Mathematics & Computer Science"],["ComputerScience","Computer Science"],["ComputerScience/machine-learning","Machine learning"],["ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning","Supervised learning and regression"],["ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression","Gaussian process regression"]],"children":[],"edges":[],"links":{"uses":[["Probability/distributions/gaussian-conditioning","Gaussian conditioning","Probability distributions",2]],"uses_total":1,"used_by":[["ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/kernel-ridge-regression-and-gblup","Kernel ridge regression and GBLUP","Supervised learning and regression",5]],"used_by_total":1},"results":[{"id":"ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/kernel-matrix","title":"Kernel matrix","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"Cslib.MachineLearning.GaussianProcessRegression.kernelMatrix","summary":"For a kernel $k : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and two families of inputs $a = (a_i)_{i \\in \\alpha}$ and $b = (b_j)_{j \\in \\beta}$ in $X$, the kernel (Gram) matrix $K(a, b)$ is the $\\alpha \\times \\beta$ real matrix with entries $K(a, b)_{ij} = k(a_i, b_j)$.","route":"../roadmap/ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/index.html#kernel-matrix"},{"id":"ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/joint-cov","title":"Joint prior covariance in Gaussian-process regression","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"Cslib.MachineLearning.GaussianProcessRegression.jointCov","summary":"The joint prior covariance of the test values `f(x*)` and the noisy training targets `y = f(x) + ε`, `ε ~ N(0, σ² I)`: `[[K(x*, x*), K(x*, x)], [K(x, x*), K(x, x) + σ² I]]`.","route":"../roadmap/ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/index.html#joint-cov"},{"id":"ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/test-part","title":"Test-value projection","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"Cslib.MachineLearning.GaussianProcessRegression.testPart","summary":"For finite index sets $\\tau$ (test inputs) and $\\nu$ (training inputs), the test-value projection is the $\\tau \\times (\\tau \\sqcup \\nu)$ real matrix with the identity matrix on the columns indexed by $\\tau$ and a zero block on the columns indexed by $\\nu$. It sends a joint vector $(u, y)$ with $u \\in \\mathbb{R}^{\\tau}$ and $y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{\\nu}$ to its block of test values $u$.","route":"../roadmap/ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/index.html#test-part"},{"id":"ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/target-part","title":"Training-target projection","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"Cslib.MachineLearning.GaussianProcessRegression.targetPart","summary":"For finite index sets $\\tau$ (test inputs) and $\\nu$ (training inputs), the training-target projection is the $\\nu \\times (\\tau \\sqcup \\nu)$ real matrix with a zero block on the columns indexed by $\\tau$ and the identity matrix on the columns indexed by $\\nu$. It sends a joint vector $(u, y)$ with $u \\in \\mathbb{R}^{\\tau}$ and $y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{\\nu}$ to its block of training targets $y$.","route":"../roadmap/ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/index.html#target-part"},{"id":"ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/is-unit-det-kernel-matrix-add","title":"Regularized kernel matrices are invertible","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"Cslib.MachineLearning.GaussianProcessRegression.isUnit_det_kernelMatrix_add","summary":"Let $k : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be a kernel and $x = (x_j)_{j \\in \\nu}$ a finite family of inputs whose kernel matrix $K = (k(x_i, x_j))_{i, j \\in \\nu}$ is positive semidefinite. Then for every $\\sigma^2 > 0$ the matrix $K + \\sigma^2 I$ has nonzero determinant, so it is invertible.","route":"../roadmap/ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/index.html#is-unit-det-kernel-matrix-add"},{"id":"ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/joint-cov-eq-add","title":"The joint covariance as a kernel matrix plus noise","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"Cslib.MachineLearning.GaussianProcessRegression.jointCov_eq_add","summary":"For a kernel $k : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$, a real number $\\sigma^2$, test inputs $x^* = (x^*_i)_{i \\in \\tau}$ and training inputs $x = (x_j)_{j \\in \\nu}$, the joint covariance of $k$, $\\sigma^2$, $x^*$ and $x$ equals $K + D$, where $K$ is the kernel matrix of the combined family $(x^*, x)$ indexed by $\\tau \\sqcup \\nu$ and $D$ is the diagonal matrix with entry $0$ at each test index and $\\sigma^2$ at each…","route":"../roadmap/ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/index.html#joint-cov-eq-add"},{"id":"ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/joint-cov-pos-semidef","title":"The joint covariance is positive semidefinite","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"Cslib.MachineLearning.GaussianProcessRegression.jointCov_posSemidef","summary":"Let $k : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be a kernel, let $x^* = (x^*_i)_{i \\in \\tau}$ and $x = (x_j)_{j \\in \\nu}$ be families of test and training inputs, and let $\\sigma^2 \\ge 0$. If the kernel matrix of the combined family $(x^*, x)$, indexed by $\\tau \\sqcup \\nu$, is positive semidefinite, then the joint covariance of $k$, $\\sigma^2$, $x^*$ and $x$ is positive semidefinite.","route":"../roadmap/ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/index.html#joint-cov-pos-semidef"},{"id":"ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/kernel-matrix-pos-semidef-train","title":"Training blocks of positive semidefinite kernel matrices","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"Cslib.MachineLearning.GaussianProcessRegression.kernelMatrix_posSemidef_train","summary":"Let $k : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be a kernel and let $x^* = (x^*_i)_{i \\in \\tau}$ and $x = (x_j)_{j \\in \\nu}$ be families of inputs. If the kernel matrix of the combined family $(x^*, x)$, indexed by $\\tau \\sqcup \\nu$, is positive semidefinite, then so is the kernel matrix $(k(x_i, x_j))_{i, j \\in \\nu}$ of $x$.","route":"../roadmap/ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/index.html#kernel-matrix-pos-semidef-train"},{"id":"ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/cond-distrib-test-given-targets","title":"The Gaussian-process posterior","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"Cslib.MachineLearning.GaussianProcessRegression.condDistrib_test_given_targets","summary":"Let $k : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be a kernel, let $x^* = (x^*_i)_{i \\in \\tau}$ (test inputs) and $x = (x_j)_{j \\in \\nu}$ (training inputs) be finite families in $X$, and write $K(a, b)$ for the kernel matrix $(k(a_i, b_j))_{i, j}$. Assume that the kernel matrix of the combined family $(x^*, x)$, indexed by $\\tau \\sqcup \\nu$, is positive semidefinite, and let $\\sigma^2 > 0$. Let $V$ be a measurable random vector…","route":"../roadmap/ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/index.html#cond-distrib-test-given-targets"}],"result_edges":[["ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/cond-distrib-test-given-targets","ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-process-regression/is-unit-det-kernel-matrix-add",0,1],["ComputerScience/machine-learning/supervised-learning/gaussian-proces